# Binary assets, marked explicitly.
#
# git guesses text vs binary, and on Windows with core.autocrlf=true a wrong
# guess rewrites line endings INSIDE a binary file -- a woff2 or a png that
# survives the round trip on the machine that committed it and is corrupt
# everywhere else. The fonts are precached by both service workers, so a
# corrupt one is an app that renders in a fallback face offline and nowhere
# else, which is close to undebuggable from a phone.
*.woff2 binary
*.png   binary
*.zip   binary
*.ico   binary

# Text that must keep LF regardless of platform: these are read by bash and by
# psql, both of which are entitled to be annoyed by CRLF.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf

# JavaScript, for a subtler reason than being annoyed.
#
# tools/embed-core.mjs splices zero-core.js into Zero.jsx and strips the CommonJS
# export on the way, by replacing the literal
# "...module.exports = ZeroCore;\n". Checked out with CRLF that string is not
# there, the replace quietly does nothing, and the export line is embedded into
# a browser bundle that has no `module`. Worse, the same mismatch makes the
# check PASS on the machine that did it -- both sides were generated the same
# wrong way -- and fail only in CI, which is the one place the LF originals are
# what gets read.
#
# So the guard that exists to stop the embedded copy drifting was itself
# defeated by a line ending. Pin the whole family rather than the two files
# involved today.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
